{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.65,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 6, "param": "n_iter"}
      ],
      "why": "Area-minimal family: 1 shared datapath with barrel shifters and 1 micro-rotation/cycle. The >=1 MSPS constraint is not binding here - at these per-family Fmax values Fmax/(N+3) is far above 1 MSPS for the whole N range, so it only has to be checked, not optimised. The whole (luts_plus_ffs, accuracy_bits) front for this spec should come from here, so it gets the majority share. data_width 10..20 brackets the accuracy boundary (output LSB 2^-(W-2) => W~11-12 is the smallest width that can possibly carry 2^-10 after rounding) and extends to the HV reference area of 1500. n_iter 6..22 brackets the CORDIC residual-rotation boundary near N~10-12 and reaches accuracy_bits well above the 10-bit HV reference. angle_guard -1..3 and frac_guard 0..2 let the search buy a little internal precision cheaply instead of brute-forcing W and N, which is where the low-area end of the front will be decided. Both rounding modes are offered because round is expected to be the cheapest way to gain ~1 bit at the output."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -1, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 5, "low": 2, "param": "k"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 6, "param": "n_iter"}
      ],
      "why": "Kept in the plan because it may be genuinely area-competitive with iterative: inside a k-group the shift amounts are constants, so it can avoid the barrel shifter that dominates iterative's LUTs, at the price of a few extra registers - exactly the trade-off that decides min luts_plus_ffs. Accuracy is bit-identical to iterative at the same (W, N, guards, rounding), so any point it wins on area is a strict front improvement and any point it loses on is dominated; the NSGA-II run will sort that out. k is held to 2..5 because throughput is already far above 1 MSPS at N=22 even with k=2, so larger k only adds chained logic without buying accuracy (accuracy depends on N, not k). Same W/N/guard ranges as iterative so the two families are compared on identical accuracy points."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only iterative and unrolled_k are permitted to this architect, and for this spec they are also the right place to look: throughput >= 1 MSPS is a very weak constraint for single-result-per-many-cycles machines at these Fmax values, so it is a feasibility check rather than a design driver. That leaves min luts_plus_ffs against max accuracy_bits, which is precisely the area/accuracy front these two low-area families define. Accuracy needs roughly W >= 11-12 and N >= 10-12 to reach max_abs_err <= 2^-10, so the ranges are centred on and just below that feasibility boundary (to map the constraint edge) and extended upward to W=20 / N=22 so the front reaches toward the HV reference area of 1500 and accuracy_bits comfortably above the 10-bit reference. Guard bits (angle_guard, frac_guard) and rounding are included at small ranges because they are the cheap knobs that move a point across the accuracy constraint without adding a data-width step. Round 1 is deliberately broad rather than narrow: with 100 evals I want to locate the feasible area front in both families and see whether unrolled_k's constant-shift structure beats iterative's barrel shifters on area, before spending the reserved later rounds on fine mapping around whatever front emerges. If round 1 shows no design with max_abs_err <= 2^-10 and throughput_msps >= 1, the next decision will be infeasible rather than relaxing the spec."
}
